</section>

<!-- INTRODUCTION SECTION -->
<section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
  <h2 id="intro-heading" class="sr-only">About GMT to AST Conversion</h2>
  <p>
    Welcome to our official <strong>GMT to AST Timezone Converter</strong>. Managing scheduling between Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) in Europe and Atlantic Standard Time (AST) across Atlantic Canada and the Caribbean requires navigating a <strong>4-hour time difference</strong>. Greenwich Mean Time functions as the universal zero baseline at Coordinated Universal Time +0 (UTC+0), while Atlantic Standard Time operates at UTC-4. As a result, Atlantic Standard Time is exactly 4 hours behind Greenwich Mean Time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 4-hour gap creates a remarkably convenient overlap for transatlantic business. When professionals in London or Dublin enjoy their lunch break at 12:00 PM GMT, colleagues in Halifax, Nova Scotia, or San Juan, Puerto Rico are starting their morning at 8:00 AM AST. This allows generous multi-hour windows for real-time collaboration every day.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our online converter utilizes native browser localization APIs backed by official IANA database rulesets (`UTC`, `America/Halifax`, and `America/Puerto_Rico`). Whether you are coordinating maritime shipping schedules across the North Atlantic, planning Caribbean resort operations, or managing financial logistics, this guide provides real-time dual clocks, interactive 24-hour schedules, meeting planners, and detailed regional context.
  </p>
</section>

