<section class="content-section">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Converting GMT to AST</h2>
  <div class="use-cases">
    <article>
      <h3>1. Confusing AST with EST</h3>
      <p>
        Atlantic Standard Time (UTC-4) is 1 hour ahead of Eastern Standard Time (EST, UTC-5). Converting to EST instead of AST will cause your calculation to be 1 hour slow.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>2. Forgetting Newfoundland's Half-Hour Offset</h3>
      <p>
        Newfoundland uses NST (UTC-3:30), which is 30 minutes ahead of AST. Do not apply AST rules to St. John's or Newfoundland destinations.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>3. Overlooking Non-DST Caribbean Islands</h3>
      <p>
        While Atlantic Canada shifts to ADT in summer, Puerto Rico and the US Virgin Islands stay on AST year-round.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

